### Description

Since #6081, `dump()` and the other value walks recurse at most `JSON_RECURSION_DEPTH_LIMIT` levels (128 by default) and continue with an explicit stack. The stack that these levels need is documented ([`JSON_RECURSION_DEPTH_LIMIT`, "Stack needed"](https://json.nlohmann.me/api/macros/json_recursion_depth_limit/#stack-needed) and [Nesting depth](https://json.nlohmann.me/features/parsing/untrusted_input/#nesting-depth)):

> The default of `128` levels is tuned for threads with ordinary stacks of a few hundred kilobytes or more. Set a lower limit if your threads have small stacks (RTOS tasks, fibers, threads of the musl C library, which has a default stack of 128 KiB) [...]

So this is not a bug: a crash on a 128 KiB thread at `-O0` is a documented configuration that needs a lower limit. But the main reason for the 288 KiB is avoidable. `dump()` is by far the most demanding operation at `-O0` (288 KiB; copy needs 160 to 208 KiB, everything else 64 KiB or less), and its cost per level comes almost entirely from one stack frame that grew sixfold since 3.12.0:

| recursive frame, `-O0` (`-fstack-usage`) | 3.12.0 | develop |
|---|---:|---:|
| Apple clang 21, `serializer::dump` / `dump_container` | 480 B | 2304 B |
| GCC 16.2 (Homebrew, macOS), `dump_container` | | 2048 B |

In 3.12.0, `dump()` of a value nested 138 levels deep ran in a 64 KiB thread (clang `-O0`, macOS) and in a 32 KiB thread (GCC 14 `-O0`, Alpine/musl). On develop it needs 288 KiB (clang) and 224 KiB (GCC 14, musl), and it crashes in a musl thread with the default 128 KiB stack. The documents of that depth are tiny: 277 bytes of text.

**Cause.** [`dump_container()`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/detail/output/serializer.hpp#L143-L258), the function that recurses once per level, has eight inlined `dump_element()` calls, six `put_literal_indent()` and two `put_literal()` calls, plus the `put_short_literal_indent()`, `put_literal()` and `put_indent()` inlined into each `put_literal_indent()`, all marked `JSON_HEDLEY_ALWAYS_INLINE` (#5878, #5883):

[`include/nlohmann/detail/output/serializer.hpp#L1400-L1410`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/detail/output/serializer.hpp#L1400-L1410)
```cpp
template<std::size_t N>
JSON_HEDLEY_ALWAYS_INLINE void put_literal_indent(const char (&s)[N], const std::size_t indent)
{
    if (JSON_HEDLEY_LIKELY(put_short_literal_indent(s, indent)))   // also always_inline
    {
        return;
    }
    put_literal(s);                                                 // always_inline
    put_indent(indent);                                             // always_inline, calls put_short_literal_indent again
}
```

At `-O0`, `always_inline` is still honoured, but the compiler does not share stack slots between the inlined copies: every copy's locals, parameters and temporaries get slots of their own in the caller's frame. That is the 2.3 KB. At `-O2` the frame is 112 B (clang) and 128 B (GCC), so optimized builds are not affected (16 KiB at depth 138).

**Since when:** 3.13.0 development (#5878, #5883 made the helpers `always_inline`; #6081 bounded the recursion). 3.12.0: the frame was 480 B, but the recursion was unbounded.
**Security:** Not a vulnerability by itself: the stack need is bounded by the limit and documented. It matters for unoptimized builds on small stacks (musl threads, RTOS tasks, fibers), where a 277-byte document crashes `dump()` at the default limit; such users must lower the limit today (documented).
**Related:** #6081 (`JSON_RECURSION_DEPTH_LIMIT`), #5387 (stack overflow in `dump()` on deep values), #5878, #5883.

**Possible fix:** keep forced inlining for optimized builds only. In `macro_scope.hpp` (with an `#undef` in `macro_unscope.hpp`):

```cpp
// forced inlining only where it is wanted for speed: at -O0, GCC and Clang give every
// inlined copy its own stack slots, which made the recursive frame of
// serializer::dump_container() about 2.3 KB
#if defined(__OPTIMIZE__) || defined(_MSC_VER)
    #define JSON_INTERNAL_SERIALIZER_INLINE JSON_HEDLEY_ALWAYS_INLINE
#else
    #define JSON_INTERNAL_SERIALIZER_INLINE inline
#endif
```

and use it for the seven `JSON_HEDLEY_ALWAYS_INLINE` functions of `serializer.hpp` (`dump_element`, `emit_pending`, `reserve_string_buffer`, `put_indent`, `put_literal_indent`, `put_short_literal_indent`, `put_literal`). GCC and Clang define `__OPTIMIZE__` at every `-O` level above `-O0` (also `-Og`, `-Os`), so optimized code is unchanged. MSVC keeps today's `__forceinline` (MSVC debug builds use `/Ob0`, which does not expand it anyway; not measured), so no new C4714 warnings.

Measured on a copy of the headers with exactly this change (macro defined in `serializer.hpp` for the test, all seven functions switched):

| | develop | with the change |
|---|---:|---:|
| `dump_container` frame, clang 21 `-O0` | 2304 B | 320 B |
| `dump_container` frame, GCC 16 `-O0` | 2048 B | 368 B |
| smallest stack for `dump()`/`dump(4)` at depth 138, arrays and objects, clang 21 `-O0` (macOS) | 288 KiB | 48 KiB |
| same, GCC 16 `-O0` (macOS) | 256 KiB | 48 KiB |
| same, GCC 14 `-O0`, Alpine 3.22 (musl), arm64 container | 224 KiB | 32 KiB |
| `dump()` at depth 138 in a 128 KiB musl thread, GCC 14 `-O0` | segmentation fault | ok |

At `-O2` (clang 21), the object code of a TU that calls `dump()` and `diff()` is identical except for the `__LINE__` constants of the assertions (checked with `otool -tv`). At `-O0`, `dump()` of `twitter.json` took 3.08 ms with and without the change (best of 7); the other files I timed were too noisy on a loaded machine to state a difference. `unit-serialization`, `unit-recursion_depth_limit` and `unit-inspection` pass at `-O0` on the copy. Tested on a copy: yes. Not an API or behaviour change; no opt-in macro needed.

Afterwards the tables in `json_recursion_depth_limit.md` and `untrusted_input.md` (the `dump()` rows, the "2.3 KiB per level" rule of thumb, and the "which limit to choose" table, where copying becomes the limiting operation at `-O0`) must be re-measured.

Further candidates, smaller and optional:

- **Copy at `-O0`** is the next largest (160 KiB for arrays, 208 KiB for objects, clang `-O0`; on musl GCC 14 `-O0` a copy of 138 nested objects still crashes in a 128 KiB thread with the change above, it needs 144 KiB). The same `-fstack-usage` survey shows which inlined helpers dominate its frames.
- **`diff()` at `-O2` on Linux**: on macOS it needs 48 KiB at depth 138 (clang 21 and GCC 16), as the docs table says, but with GCC 14 on Alpine/musl (arm64) it needs 96 KiB for nested objects; the recursive `diff_unequal()` frame is 656 B there (448 B with GCC 16 on macOS, 368 B with clang). The frame holds the object branch's `diff_target_values` (16 inline pointers, [`json.hpp#L7596`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L7596)) and the `added` vector even when the level is an array. Moving the object branch of [`diff_unequal()`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/5bad6c518bf6663b69f505511a19c98c91f77c3a/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L7900) into a function of its own (`JSON_HEDLEY_NEVER_INLINE`, still recursive) would keep arrays from paying for it; objects would not get smaller. Needs a benchmark (acceptance: no row more than 1 % worse). The docs table should get a note that Linux/GCC needed twice the macOS value here.

**Test:** a stack-size test is not portable (pthreads only, and sanitizers and coverage builds have much larger frames), so I would not add one to the unit tests. Verification for the PR:

1. Frame size: `clang++ -std=c++11 -O0 -fstack-usage -I include stack.cpp` and `grep dump_container *.su` (expect about 320 B instead of 2304 B); same with `g++`. Alternatively `-Wframe-larger-than=1024` on a TU that calls `dump()` must not name `dump_container`.
2. Stack: the program below with `./stack dump 138 <kib>` (and `obj-dump`, `dump4`), searching for the smallest `kib` in steps of 16, before and after.
3. `-O2` unchanged: compare `otool -tv` / `objdump -d` of the same TU before and after.

Optionally a CI-only check (not a unit test): a target in `cmake/ci.cmake` that compiles one TU at `-O0` with `-fstack-usage` and fails if `dump_container` exceeds, say, 1024 B.

### Reproduction steps

1. Save the example below as `stack.cpp`.
2. `clang++ -std=c++11 -O0 -I include stack.cpp -o stack -fstack-usage`
3. `./stack dump 138 128; echo $?` (crashes), `./stack dump 138 288` (ok); `grep dump_container stack.su` (on macOS the `.su` file is named after the output).
4. On musl: `docker run --rm -v "$PWD":/work -w /work alpine:3.22 sh -c 'apk add -q g++; g++ -std=c++11 -O0 -I include stack.cpp -o stack_musl && ./stack_musl dump 138 128'`.

### Expected vs. actual results

- **Expected:** a recursive frame of a few hundred bytes per level at `-O0`, as in 3.12.0 (480 B), so that the default limit of 128 levels fits a 128 KiB thread in an unoptimized build.
- **Actual:** 2304 B per level (clang `-O0`); `dump()` of a value nested 138 levels deep needs 288 KiB and crashes in a 128 KiB thread.

### Minimal code example

```cpp
// Runs one operation on a value nested `depth` levels deep in a thread with a stack of `kib` KiB.
// usage: ./stack [obj-]<dump|dump4|diff|copy> <depth> <kib>; prints "ok" on success (a stack overflow kills the process)
#include <cstdlib>
#include <cstring>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>

static nlohmann::json a, b;
static const char* op = "dump";
static std::size_t result = 0;

static void* run(void*)
{
    if (std::strcmp(op, "dump") == 0) { result = a.dump().size(); }
    else if (std::strcmp(op, "dump4") == 0) { result = a.dump(4).size(); }
    else if (std::strcmp(op, "diff") == 0) { result = nlohmann::json::diff(a, b).size(); }
    else if (std::strcmp(op, "copy") == 0) { nlohmann::json* c = new nlohmann::json(a); result = c->size(); /* leaked: destruction is not measured */ }
    return nullptr;
}

static bool objects = false;
static nlohmann::json nested(int depth, int leaf)
{
    nlohmann::json v = leaf;
    for (int i = 0; i < depth; ++i)
    {
        nlohmann::json outer = objects ? nlohmann::json::object() : nlohmann::json::array();
        if (objects) { outer["k"] = std::move(v); } else { outer.push_back(std::move(v)); }
        v = std::move(outer);
    }
    return v;
}

int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
    if (argc < 4) { return 2; }
    op = argv[1];
    if (std::strncmp(op, "obj-", 4) == 0) { objects = true; op += 4; }
    const int depth = std::atoi(argv[2]);
    const std::size_t kib = static_cast<std::size_t>(std::atoi(argv[3]));
    a = nested(depth, 1);
    b = nested(depth, 2);
    pthread_attr_t attr;
    pthread_attr_init(&attr);
    if (pthread_attr_setstacksize(&attr, kib * 1024) != 0) { std::cout << "cannot set stack size\n"; return 2; }
    pthread_t thread;
    pthread_create(&thread, &attr, run, nullptr);
    pthread_join(thread, nullptr);
    std::cout << op << " depth " << depth << " stack " << kib << " KiB: ok (" << result << ")\n";
}
```

### Error messages

Smallest stack (steps of 16 KiB) at depth 138 = default limit + 10, develop:

```
clang_O0 dump depth=138: 288 KiB
clang_O0 dump4 depth=138: 288 KiB
clang_O0 diff depth=138: 64 KiB
clang_O0 copy depth=138: 160 KiB
clang_O0 obj-copy depth=138: 208 KiB
gcc_O0 dump depth=138: 256 KiB
gcc_O0 copy depth=138: 112 KiB
clang_O2 dump depth=138: 16 KiB
clang_O2 diff depth=138: 48 KiB
gcc_O2 dump depth=138: 16 KiB
gcc_O2 diff depth=138: 48 KiB
```

3.12.0, clang `-O0`:

```
v312_O0 dump depth=138: 64 KiB
v312_O0 obj-dump depth=138: 64 KiB
```

Alpine 3.22 (musl), GCC 14.2 `-O0`, 128 KiB thread, depth 138:

```
develop          dump: Segmentation fault (core dumped)
develop      obj-dump: Segmentation fault (core dumped)
with the fix     dump: dump depth 138 stack 128 KiB: ok (277)
with the fix obj-dump: dump depth 138 stack 128 KiB: ok (829)
3.12.0           dump: dump depth 138 stack 128 KiB: ok (277)
dev_O0 dump depth=138: 224 KiB
fix_O0 dump depth=138: 32 KiB
v312_O0 dump depth=138: 32 KiB
dev_O2 obj-diff depth=138: 96 KiB
```

### Compiler and operating system

Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.4.1.1) and Homebrew GCC 16.2.0, macOS 27.0.1, arm64; GCC 14.2.0 on Alpine 3.22 (musl) in an arm64 container (colima)

### Library version

develop (5bad6c518b); 3.12.0: not affected in this form (frame 480 B at `-O0`, but no recursion bound at all)

